<node id="paper::broad_444fa95d3a7db533ade0::claim_reconstruction_free_dual_sites">
  <data key="d4">MechanismClaim</data>
  <data key="d5">Strong atomic metal–support interaction stabilizes the reactive atomic Ru and Ni sites, enabling a steady reconstruction-free surface for bifunctional HER/OER catalysis.</data>
  <data key="d18">mechanistic_interpretation</data>
  <data key="d19">Strong atomic metal–support interaction stabilizes the reactive atomic Ru and Ni sites, enabling a steady reconstruction-free surface for bifunctional HER/OER catalysis.</data>
  <data key="d20">mixed</data>
  <data key="d6">The abstract attributes reconstruction-free behavior to stabilization of the dangling-bond-rich surface and the reactive Ru and Ni sites; this interpretation is supported by in situ XAS and operando Raman investigations.</data>
  <data key="d7">["claim_reconstruction_free_dual_sites"]</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
  <data key="d9">broad_mechanism_abstract</data>
  <data key="d10">broad_abstract_support</data>
  <data key="d11">True</data>
  <data key="d12">abstract</data>
  <data key="d13">type: MechanismClaim
label: Strong atomic metal–support interaction stabilizes the reactive atomic Ru and Ni sites, enabling a steady reconstruction-free surface for bifunctional HER/OER catalysis.
source depth: abstract
epistemic use: exploration/bridge; verify before target-domain premise use
description: The abstract attributes reconstruction-free behavior to stabilization of the dangling-bond-rich surface and the reactive Ru and Ni sites; this interpretation is supported by in situ XAS and operando Raman investigations.</data>
  <data key="d14">broad_444fa95d3a7db533ade0</data>
  <data key="d15">claim_reconstruction_free_dual_sites</data>
  <data key="d16">["broad_444fa95d3a7db533ade0"]</data>
  <data key="d17">paper_local</data>
</node>
<node id="paper::broad_444fa95d3a7db533ade0::claim_site_specific_stabilization">
  <data key="d4">MechanismClaim</data>
  <data key="d5">Atomic Ru stabilizes the Ni site during anodic OER, increasing its structural tolerance toward bond constriction and structural distortion and contributing to improved OER performance.</data>
  <data key="d18">mechanistic_interpretation</data>
  <data key="d19">Atomic Ru stabilizes the Ni site during anodic OER, increasing its structural tolerance toward bond constriction and structural distortion and contributing to improved OER performance.</data>
  <data key="d20">mixed</data>
  <data key="d6">The abstract explicitly describes stabilization of the Ni site by the atomic Ru site during oxidation of Ni.</data>
